=======================================================================
 olele -- commands you run from the repo root
=======================================================================

./proj is the single entry point: one verb per thing you do. Run
"./proj --help" for the full list. It acts on ONE variant per invocation,
chosen by the VARIANT env var, whose default lives in exactly one place --
bootenv.sh -- and is currently "marathon". Override it per command:

    ./proj -v sizif run

or once per shell:

    source ./bootenv.sh                # also puts the Xilinx tools on PATH
    export VARIANT=sizif

Sourcing bootenv.sh also defines shortcuts, so the common verbs are one
word from any directory: build, compile, run, stop, clean -- plus "proj"
itself for the rest (proj pc status, proj net show).

    sizif     AXI-Lite architecture, frozen reference
    marathon  DMA / AXI-Stream architecture, active development

Only one variant can own the board at a time -- they flash the same part.
Both also use TCP port 5001, so stop one before starting the other.


-----------------------------------------------------------------------
 EVERY DAY
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

  source ./bootenv.sh              Once per shell, before anything else.
                                   SOURCE it, do not execute it.

  ./proj net apply                 Once per BOOT, before any throughput
                                   work. Kernel state, so a reboot loses
                                   it and the stream goes unstable again.

  ./proj run                       Flash + run the board, and start the
                                   PC app. Needs "proj build" done first.
                                   --no-board / --no-pc do just one half.

  ./proj stop                      Stop the PC app and the serial console.
                                   The firmware keeps running until reset,
                                   reflashed or power-cycled.


-----------------------------------------------------------------------
 BUILDING
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

  ./proj build                Whole stack: Vivado bitstream + .xsa, Vitis
                              platform + firmware ELF, relay binary + venv.
                              Slow. Builds only, does not flash or start
                              anything.
                                --stage vivado|vitis|pc   just one part
                                --skip-bitstream          reuse the .bit

  ./proj compile              Fast path: firmware C and the C++ relay only.
                              Use after editing app/src/*.c or
                              tcp_server_app.cpp. Skips Vivado synth and
                              Vitis platform creation.

  ./proj clean                Wipe this variant's build/ dirs. Another
                              variant is left alone so a slow Vivado
                              rebuild is not triggered by accident.
                                --what vivado|vitis|pc    just one part

  Rule of thumb: "proj build" only when the bitstream or the Vitis platform
  itself changed. Otherwise "proj compile". Neither is needed for a
  Python-only change -- "proj pc restart-client" covers that.


-----------------------------------------------------------------------
 PC SIDE ONLY  (no reflash -- passthrough to pc_app/$VARIANT/system.sh)
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

  ./proj pc start
             stop
             restart           both relay and client
             restart-client    after editing config.py, net.py, plot.py...
             restart-server    after editing tcp_server_app.cpp
             status            what is running
             logs              tail both logs

  restart-client is the one to prefer while experimenting: it leaves the
  relay up, so the board's TCP connection survives and the board-side [S]
  stats stay continuous.

  Panel fields (send rate, chunk size, frame rate, trigger, heart rate...)
  are live -- they need NO restart at all.


 OFFLINE ANALYSIS  (no board, no relay, no client)
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

  Press "Log buffer" in the client's plot bar to capture the on-screen
  window. It writes a pair of files under pc_app/$VARIANT/build/logs/:
  the samples, and a sidecar with every setting that produced them.

  SAT (Static Analysis Tool) reads them offline. Either click the "SAT"
  button next to "Log buffer" in the client, or run it directly:

  cd pc_app/marathon
  source build/venv/bin/activate
  ./sat.py                        window, on the newest dump
  ./sat.py FILE.csv               ...on a particular one
  ./sat.py --list                 what dumps exist
  ./sat.py --no-plot              numbers only (scripting, or no display)

  Spectra, tone attenuation, and --model iir to score the recorded board
  output against a bit-accurate model of axi_tdm_filter.vhd.

  GOTCHA: with the ECG enabled, its harmonics are stronger than an
  injected tone, so the peak lands at ~6 Hz (120 bpm) and the reported
  attenuation is the filter's response THERE. Set "Peak from" to 40 Hz
  (or --peak-fmin 40) to measure the tone instead.

  The live client deliberately has no spectrum view -- an FFT on a rolling
  buffer costs something every frame forever to answer a question that is
  not live. Log, then measure.


-----------------------------------------------------------------------
 NETWORK TUNING / DIAGNOSIS
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

  ./proj net apply       Apply. Needs sudo. Existing TCP connections do
                         NOT pick it up -- restart the PC side after.
  ./proj net show        Is it applied? No sudo needed. FIRST THING TO
                         CHECK if throughput is fluctuating.
  ./proj net loss        Live packet loss to the board. ~0.85% is the
                         known standing figure; rising means you are
                         pushing past what the board's rx path absorbs.
  ./proj net revert      Remove the flags.


-----------------------------------------------------------------------
 WHEN SOMETHING LOOKS WRONG -- CHECK IN THIS ORDER
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

  1. ./proj net show               untuned after a reboot is the most
                                   likely cause of "it fluctuates"
  2. ./proj pc logs                the client line tells you WHERE:
                                     (dropped N late)  -> local PC stall
                                     (N send-stalls)   -> far end pushing
                                                          back
                                   both near zero and still short of the
                                   setpoint -> the limit is elsewhere
  3. the board's PuTTY console     [E] lines, and the [S] rate; tx= shows
                                   only when it differs from rx, and +/-1
                                   is just a window-boundary straddle
  4. ./proj net loss               link health

  Note the board has been the innocent party in every problem so far.
  Suspect the PC and the link first.
